1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Here's a beaker of purple juice from a

2
00:00:02,080 --> 00:00:03,120
red cabbage.

3
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,200
Add a few drops of acid, and it

4
00:00:05,320 --> 00:00:06,000
turns red.

5
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,560
Add a base, and it turns green.

6
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:11,440
Add acid again, and the red comes back.

7
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:14,480
So can you make a working indicator from

8
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:15,440
a vegetable?

9
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:17,200
And how would you show that its colour

10
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,520
change really runs both ways?

11
00:00:20,734 --> 00:00:24,173
The purple comes from pigments called anthocyanins, stored

12
00:00:24,374 --> 00:00:26,893
inside the cabbage's cells, and they dissolve in

13
00:00:26,974 --> 00:00:27,693
water.

14
00:00:28,014 --> 00:00:29,933
Each one behaves like H In from the

15
00:00:30,014 --> 00:00:33,133
indicator video: a weak acid whose forms have

16
00:00:33,294 --> 00:00:34,573
different colours.

17
00:00:34,694 --> 00:00:37,133
But each can lose more than one proton,

18
00:00:37,174 --> 00:00:38,893
and the extract is a mix of several

19
00:00:38,974 --> 00:00:41,533
of them, so it passes through several colours,

20
00:00:41,614 --> 00:00:43,933
blending gradually, not just two.

21
00:00:45,134 --> 00:00:47,613
To prepare it, your teacher chops about twenty

22
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:49,693
grams of cabbage and covers it with a

23
00:00:49,734 --> 00:00:52,653
hundred millilitres of hot water for ten minutes.

24
00:00:52,974 --> 00:00:55,693
Heat damages the membranes in the cabbage cells,

25
00:00:55,774 --> 00:00:58,093
the thin layers that hold the pigment in,

26
00:00:58,174 --> 00:01:00,173
so the pigment leaks out faster.

27
00:01:00,494 --> 00:01:02,653
Then filter off the solids, so you're looking

28
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:05,053
at the solution, not bits of leaf, and

29
00:01:05,134 --> 00:01:05,773
let it cool.

30
00:01:06,334 --> 00:01:08,813
Record its starting colour: purple.

31
00:01:10,034 --> 00:01:12,953
Next, find out what each colour means.

32
00:01:13,234 --> 00:01:15,553
Put about twenty drops of solutions of known

33
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:18,433
pH, from two to twelve, into a row

34
00:01:18,514 --> 00:01:20,113
of wells on a white tile.

35
00:01:20,674 --> 00:01:23,073
Buffers work well, checked with a pH meter.

36
00:01:23,714 --> 00:01:26,273
Then add three drops of extract to every

37
00:01:26,354 --> 00:01:26,993
well.

38
00:01:27,314 --> 00:01:29,473
Colour depth depends on how much pigment is

39
00:01:29,594 --> 00:01:32,353
there, and you're comparing colour, so the amount

40
00:01:32,594 --> 00:01:33,793
must stay the same.

41
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,760
Here's a typical result: red at pH two,

42
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:42,200
pink at four, violet at six, purple at

43
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:45,880
seven, blue at eight, blue-green at ten, and

44
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,400
green at twelve.

45
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:49,600
Your batch may differ a little.

46
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:53,080
Notice there isn't one colour change but several,

47
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,960
each spread across a couple of pH units.

48
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,040
And your chart is only as precise as

49
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:01,240
its spacing: wells two units apart give you

50
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,240
pH to within a unit or so.

51
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:07,586
Now test the extract in three wells: dilute

52
00:02:07,747 --> 00:02:11,906
hydrochloric acid, distilled water, and dilute sodium hydroxide.

53
00:02:12,547 --> 00:02:13,426
Why the water?

54
00:02:13,907 --> 00:02:16,546
Adding any liquid dilutes the pigment, so the

55
00:02:16,627 --> 00:02:17,746
colour gets paler.

56
00:02:18,227 --> 00:02:20,546
The water well shows that paler purple.

57
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:23,426
So if a well turns red or green,

58
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:25,746
the acid or base is causing it, not

59
00:02:25,827 --> 00:02:26,626
dilution.

60
00:02:27,834 --> 00:02:29,353
Now the key test.

61
00:02:29,594 --> 00:02:31,833
Put two millilitres of extract in a small

62
00:02:31,914 --> 00:02:34,873
beaker and add acid until it's red.

63
00:02:34,954 --> 00:02:38,393
Then add sodium hydroxide, drop by drop, until

64
00:02:38,474 --> 00:02:40,313
it just turns green.

65
00:02:40,514 --> 00:02:42,953
Then acid again, until it's red.

66
00:02:43,034 --> 00:02:46,633
Base removes hydronium, so the pigment's equilibrium shifts

67
00:02:46,674 --> 00:02:49,033
to the forms that have lost protons, and

68
00:02:49,114 --> 00:02:50,953
acid shifts it back.

69
00:02:51,034 --> 00:02:53,113
Run the cycle three times, in the same

70
00:02:53,194 --> 00:02:53,673
beaker.

71
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,460
Why the same sample?

72
00:02:56,740 --> 00:02:59,380
Fresh wells only show each colour separately.

73
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:02,100
One sample going back and forth shows the

74
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:04,060
reaction runs both ways.

75
00:03:04,340 --> 00:03:06,580
Three cycles check that you didn't misread a

76
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:07,620
single return.

77
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:10,260
The colour gets paler each time, because the

78
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:13,140
sample keeps getting diluted, but the same two

79
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:14,740
colours keep returning.

80
00:03:14,980 --> 00:03:17,700
That's evidence the pigment isn't being used up.

81
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:20,980
But a colour that doesn't come back is

82
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:22,180
a warning.

83
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,580
If a green sample sits in strong base,

84
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:27,060
like well C, for a long time, it

85
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:29,780
can turn yellow, and acid may not bring

86
00:03:29,820 --> 00:03:30,980
the red back.

87
00:03:31,060 --> 00:03:33,540
That's a different kind of reaction: the pigment

88
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:36,820
molecules change shape and start to break down.

89
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:39,380
So add base only until it just turns

90
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:41,940
green, and run the cycle straight away.

91
00:03:43,167 --> 00:03:44,366
Now use your chart.

92
00:03:44,847 --> 00:03:48,686
Vinegar turns the extract pink-red: somewhere between pH

93
00:03:48,767 --> 00:03:49,806
two and four.

94
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:53,246
An ammonia cleaner turns it green: about eleven

95
00:03:53,327 --> 00:03:54,046
or twelve.

96
00:03:54,607 --> 00:03:56,726
Check each against a pH meter.

97
00:03:57,007 --> 00:03:59,486
But for a titration, cabbage is a poor

98
00:03:59,567 --> 00:04:00,286
choice.

99
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:03,726
Its colour changes gradually, in steps, right across

100
00:04:03,807 --> 00:04:06,846
the scale, and a titration needs one sharp

101
00:04:06,927 --> 00:04:08,526
change inside the jump.

102
00:04:09,734 --> 00:04:11,013
What are the variables?

103
00:04:11,494 --> 00:04:13,573
You change the pH of the solution, and

104
00:04:13,614 --> 00:04:14,893
you observe the colour.

105
00:04:15,174 --> 00:04:17,893
Keep the extract batch, the number of drops,

106
00:04:17,934 --> 00:04:20,133
the volume of solution and the lighting the

107
00:04:20,214 --> 00:04:20,973
same.

108
00:04:21,334 --> 00:04:24,053
Validity: one batch of extract and the water

109
00:04:24,134 --> 00:04:26,213
well mean the colour change comes from the

110
00:04:26,294 --> 00:04:27,253
pH alone.

111
00:04:27,734 --> 00:04:31,653
Reliability: repeat wells, and repeat cycles, give the

112
00:04:31,734 --> 00:04:32,613
same colours.

113
00:04:33,094 --> 00:04:36,053
Accuracy: a colour estimate is good to about

114
00:04:36,134 --> 00:04:38,693
a pH unit, and a calibrated meter does

115
00:04:38,934 --> 00:04:39,653
much better.

116
00:04:40,867 --> 00:04:43,266
Before you start, the risk assessment.

117
00:04:43,747 --> 00:04:45,906
The hot water can scald and the knife

118
00:04:45,987 --> 00:04:48,866
can cut, so your teacher prepares and cools

119
00:04:48,947 --> 00:04:49,666
the extract.

120
00:04:50,147 --> 00:04:52,466
The point one zero moles per litre acid

121
00:04:52,547 --> 00:04:55,666
is low hazard, but the sodium hydroxide sits

122
00:04:55,827 --> 00:04:58,346
just under the irritant limit, so treat a

123
00:04:58,387 --> 00:05:01,906
splash as harmful: wear safety glasses and rinse

124
00:05:01,987 --> 00:05:03,826
any splash with plenty of water.

125
00:05:04,307 --> 00:05:07,426
The extract stains skin and clothes, and nothing

126
00:05:07,507 --> 00:05:08,626
here gets tasted.

127
00:05:09,834 --> 00:05:14,073
Three words for this practical: steep, chart, cycle.

128
00:05:14,714 --> 00:05:17,353
Steep the cabbage to make the extract, chart

129
00:05:17,434 --> 00:05:20,393
its colours against known pH, and cycle one

130
00:05:20,474 --> 00:05:22,953
sample from acid to base and back to

131
00:05:23,034 --> 00:05:24,633
show the change is reversible.

132
00:05:25,834 --> 00:05:26,473
Your turn.

133
00:05:27,114 --> 00:05:29,513
A student adds three drops of extract to

134
00:05:29,594 --> 00:05:33,993
three wells: dilute hydrochloric acid, distilled water, and

135
00:05:34,074 --> 00:05:36,073
dilute sodium hydroxide.

136
00:05:36,394 --> 00:05:39,673
They turn red, a paler purple, and green.

137
00:05:39,994 --> 00:05:42,633
Explain why she included the water well, and

138
00:05:42,834 --> 00:05:44,473
describe a test that would show the colour

139
00:05:44,554 --> 00:05:46,153
change is reversible.

140
00:05:46,474 --> 00:05:47,833
Pause here to answer.

141
00:05:58,234 --> 00:06:00,953
The water well is the control: it shows

142
00:06:01,034 --> 00:06:04,393
that dilution alone only makes the purple paler.

143
00:06:04,714 --> 00:06:06,153
So the red and green come from the

144
00:06:06,314 --> 00:06:07,593
acid and base.

145
00:06:07,914 --> 00:06:11,353
For reversibility, take one sample, add base until

146
00:06:11,434 --> 00:06:14,073
it's green, then acid until it's red, and

147
00:06:14,154 --> 00:06:15,633
repeat three times.

148
00:06:15,914 --> 00:06:18,713
Testing again in fresh wells only shows each

149
00:06:18,794 --> 00:06:19,833
colour separately.

150
00:06:21,034 --> 00:06:21,873
One more.

151
00:06:22,154 --> 00:06:25,353
Her colour chart reads: red at two, pink

152
00:06:25,434 --> 00:06:29,193
at four, violet at six, purple at seven,

153
00:06:29,274 --> 00:06:32,473
blue at eight, blue-green at ten and green

154
00:06:32,554 --> 00:06:33,633
at twelve.

155
00:06:34,074 --> 00:06:37,113
A baking soda solution turns the extract blue,

156
00:06:37,194 --> 00:06:39,193
with a slight green tint.

157
00:06:39,314 --> 00:06:42,073
Estimate its pH, and assess the accuracy of

158
00:06:42,154 --> 00:06:45,513
this method compared with a calibrated pH meter.

159
00:06:45,594 --> 00:06:46,873
Pause here to answer.

160
00:06:57,300 --> 00:06:59,540
The colour sits between the blue at eight

161
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:02,900
and the blue-green at ten, closer to blue:

162
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:04,100
about eight to nine.

163
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:06,740
That's a range, not a value.

164
00:07:06,980 --> 00:07:09,460
The chart's steps are two units wide, and

165
00:07:09,540 --> 00:07:11,780
the colour is judged by eye, so it's

166
00:07:11,860 --> 00:07:14,420
less accurate than a calibrated meter, which reads

167
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:16,020
eight point three four here.

168
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:19,620
Writing exactly eight point zero claims more than

169
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:20,900
a colour can show.

170
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:25,380
So: steep and filter the extract, chart its

171
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:28,660
colours against known pH with equal drops, use

172
00:07:28,740 --> 00:07:31,460
a water control, and cycle one sample to

173
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:33,060
show the change is reversible.

174
00:07:34,180 --> 00:07:36,740
Next: what a whole titration curve, and a

175
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:38,740
conductivity graph, can tell you.

176
00:07:39,967 --> 00:07:41,406
Here are the key notes.

177
00:07:41,647 --> 00:07:43,566
Pause the video if you need more time

178
00:07:43,647 --> 00:07:44,446
to copy them.
